.TH QUADRF-JTAG 1 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-jtag \- control the QuadRF MAX285x front-end
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-jtag
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-init
.br
.B quadrf-jtag
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-\-tx " spec"
.br
.B quadrf-jtag
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-\-rx " spec"
.br
.B quadrf-jtag
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-\-status " rx|tx"
.br
.B quadrf-jtag
.RI [ options ]
.BR read " | " write
.I addr
.RI [ value ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Programs the two MAX285x transceivers on the QuadRF board through the JTAG
ioctls of the CSI driver. The driver must be loaded and the FPGA programmed,
which
.BR quadrf-load (8)
does at boot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-init
Initialise both chips and leave them in standby. Also
.B \-\-max2850
and
.B \-\-max2851
for the transmit and receive chip on their own.
.TP
.BI \-\-tx " spec"
.TQ
.BI \-\-rx " spec"
Enter transmit or receive mode and program the parameters in
.IR spec ,
a comma-separated list of key=value pairs:
.BI antennas= mask
(0xF is all four),
.BI bw= MHz
(transmit: 20 or 40; receive: any positive value, default 40),
.BI freq= MHz ,
.BI gain= 0..63 ,
.BI agc= dBFS
(receive only),
.BI pol= rhcp|lhcp
(receive only, default rhcp) and
.BI interleave= 1|0
(receive only, four-channel interleaved mode, default 0). Pass
.B off
instead of a spec to return the chip to standby.
.TP
.BI \-\-status " rx|tx"
Print the full register state of the selected chip.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-device " path"
Device node. Default:
.IR /dev/csi_stream0 .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-setup
Do not call CSI_IOC_JTAG_SETUP.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Do not call CSI_IOC_JTAG_RELEASE on exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Receive at 2.45 GHz on all four antennas, interleaved:
.PP
.nf
quadrf-jtag --rx antennas=0xF,freq=2450,bw=40,gain=40,interleave=1
.fi
.PP
Put the transmitter back into standby:
.PP
.nf
quadrf-jtag --tx off
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf (1),
.BR quadrf-load (8)
